#{[#edb/ref 17592186048150 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069909} :activity/id #uuid "fe4f8c5a-9202-5761-89a0-f7bd470edcd6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070035 :tutorial/content "How do we evaluate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} \\textrm{d}x?$\n\nFirst of all, note that the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nIf we can figure out the partial fractions, then we can integrate each of the partial fractions using the natural logarithm.\n\nSo, let's compute the partial fractions. If we multiply the above equation by $(x+2)(x-3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =A(x-3)+B(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above must hold for all values of $x.$ Plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)-1 & =A(-2-3)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -5 & =A(-5)+B(0) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-1 & =A(3-3)+B(3+2) \\\\ 5 & =A(0)+B(5) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{1}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can integrate each of the partial fractions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068584 :question/id #uuid "f374b567-9a69-57ae-88eb-644ebfc451d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-506" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068586 :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Partial Fractions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068973}] [#edb/ref 17592186048115 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084071} :activity/id #uuid "075e6353-42e9-5465-b0d4-8a611aacb2c3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084525 :tutorial/content "An **improper integral** is an integral where at least one limit of integration is unbounded. \n\nFor example, the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nis an improper integral because the upper limit is unbounded (i.e., is infinite).\n\nThis improper integral can be interpreted as the shaded area under the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ shown below. \n\n![](images/38/387754335eb4fe9cc1da3994c74d184fb7a76f44d06e7cdd843ecce043fc790f.png)\n\nTo compute an improper integral, we need to compute the limit of the integral as the upper limit approaches infinity. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{a\\to\\infty} \\int_2^a \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo compute this improper integral, we start by integrating as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([-\\frac{1}{a}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the limits as $a\\to \\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{a}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac12.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083001 :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083002 :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084527 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, calculating an improper integral involves taking a limit. \n\nWhen computing this limit, we typically have two possible cases:\n\n- If the limit exists, as it has in the previous examples, we say that the integral is **convergent**.\n\n- However, if the limit does not exist or is infinite, we say that the integral is **divergent**.\n\nLet's discuss a concrete example of a divergent improper integral. Consider the following:\n\n$$\nI = \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to work out $I$ by integrating over $x\\in[1,a]$ as usual and taking the limit as $a$ tends to positive infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}|x|]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([\\operatorname{ln}a]-[\\operatorname{ln}1]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([\\operatorname{ln}a]-[0]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}a) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit of $I$ as $a\\rightarrow \\infty$ is infinite. Therefore, the integral $I$ is *divergent*." :tutorial/title "Divergent Improper Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083003 :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082895 :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084083}}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083448}]}
